From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 04:38:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Python3.11+: Optimization: Call methods of objects without a
 dict directly

Since Python 3.11, the generic attribute lookup was disabled for all
types, because it does not handle the instance dictionary those manage.
That meant every method call created a bound method first. Objects
without any instance dictionary, e.g. "dict", "list", "str" and classes
with "__slots__", do not have that problem, so they use it again, like
CPython does with "LOAD_ATTR_METHOD_NO_DICT".

Methods of built-in types are now called with the object as first
argument, instead of creating a bound method, and calls of well known
method names, e.g. "get" or "append", on values of unknown type use the
combined method call, as other names already did.

For 300000 calls, "dict.get" takes 5.3 instead of 6.7 ms, "list.append"
6.5 instead of 8.1 ms, and a method of a class with "__slots__" 5.8
instead of 6.7 ms.

Co-authored-by: wtfyd-agent[bot] <328989958+wtfyd-agent[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
Signed-off-by: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
